<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Microsoft recommends checking another device on the same network as part of troubleshooting. That comparison is a clue, not proof that a particular device needs replacing. Make sure you are comparing the same home Wi-Fi rather than a phone's mobile connection. <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/experience/connectivity-networking/fix-wi-fi-connection-issues-in-windows">See Microsoft's troubleshooting guidance</a>.</p>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Do not spend an afternoon changing everything. Two or three clear observations are more useful than a long list of adjustments whose effects you cannot separate.</p>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">For a step-by-step comparison and help describing the result, read <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="/guides/wifi-one-device-or-all/">Wi-Fi trouble on one device or every device?</a></p>
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="tests"><span aria-hidden="true">03</span>When a speed test is useful</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">A connection test gives you something to compare. nbn suggests testing beside the router, comparing with an Ethernet connection to the router where practical, and testing Wi-Fi in other rooms. Differences between rooms can point toward a coverage or placement issue. <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="https://www.nbnco.com.au/learn/optimisation/what-to-look-for-in-a-wifi-router">Read nbn's test and interpretation guidance</a>.</p>
{% call note("care", "Take care", "Keep the comparison safe") %}<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Use the test your internet provider directs you to, record its name, and keep the device and test consistent. Note the time, room and whether the connection was Wi-Fi or Ethernet. Keep the units shown with the result. Do not move unfamiliar cabling or connect directly to an nbn box to follow this article; skip a wired comparison if you are unsure.</p>{% endcall %}
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Treat the result as an observation from that moment. Avoid turning one number into “the router is broken” or “we need a faster plan”. Bring your notes to the provider or technician so the result can be considered with the symptoms.</p>
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="diary"><span aria-hidden="true">04</span>Keep a short dropout diary</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Copy this into a note on your own device or onto paper:</p>
{% call note("info", "Try this", "Your dropout diary") %}
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">At [time], I was using [device] in [room] for [activity]. The problem lasted about [duration]. Another device [had the same problem / worked / was not checked]. I saw [connection message]. This has happened [pattern]. The test I used was [name], and its result was [value and units].</p>
{% endcall %}
{% call note("care", "Take care", "Keep private information out of your notes") %}<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">You can leave any field blank. You do not need to include passwords, authentication codes, account numbers or private documents. If you share a screenshot, check what else it reveals first.</p>{% endcall %}
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="pattern"><span aria-hidden="true">05</span>What to do with the pattern</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">If the problem follows a room, mention that. If it affects several devices together, say so. If only one program fails, name it. These are useful starting points for investigation; none obliges you to buy equipment.</p>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">If your observations point to a coverage or home-layout question rather than an unexplained dropout, continue to <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="/guides/need-mesh-wifi-access-point-router-position/">choose between mesh Wi-Fi, another access point and router position</a>. That guide owns the design and upgrade decision; this one remains the place to describe symptoms.</p>
{% call note("care", "Take care", "Know when to pause") %}<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">For service outages or continuing internet-service concerns, contact your provider. For uncertainty about your home setup, a scoped diagnosis can help separate service, router, placement, interference and coverage questions. Avoid factory resets or disabling security settings just to see what happens. If you cannot confidently reverse a change, pause and ask for help.</p>{% endcall %}
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">If ordinary computer tasks also feel slow, our <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="https://nakedtech.au/guides/slow-computer-fix-upgrade-replace/">fix, upgrade or replace guide</a> can help you organise that separate question.</p>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">For a side-by-side comparison, read <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="/guides/slow-computer-or-internet/">Is your computer slow, or is the internet slow?</a>.</p>

